    "q-124551": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124551",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14273134/assets/2464c37e0595ae5f3c9863b9fb9858dfa2335ef41f9dd5471d52c559b82d13ba.png)\n\nConsider the rectangle $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nx=u+v-1,\\,y=v-u+1.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the image of $Δ$ in the $xy$-plane under the action of $T?$",
      "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v-1 \\\\ v-u+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the boundary of $Δ$ to the boundary of the image. So, to find the image of $Δ,$ we find the images of each side.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14273134/assets/c75511b233a5d93110930d86bf19d3e2c2a347319374f2adbbe7504f9aab5982.png)\n\n          \n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing all vertices of our square:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertices of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-1,1),(1,-1),(2,0),$ and $(0,2).$\n\nDrawing this region in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14273134/assets/5bc42b39318774f9a393916bac899b9440ab86daa4bde637812e08e26431786d.png)\n\n          \n\nFinally, since $det(A)=2>0,$ this transformation is orientation-preserving.",
