    "q-124326": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124326",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=u-1,\\,y=u-v-2.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $det(A)=-1$\n\nIII. The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving",
      "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-1 \\\\ u-v-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 0=-1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $det(A)<0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is not orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
