    "q-124198": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124198",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the affine transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=v-3u-1,\\,y=2u-v+3.\n$$\n\nLet $A$ be the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $det(A)=-7$\n\nIII. The transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving",
      "worked_solution": "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nLet's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, notice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-3u-1 \\\\ 2u-v+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3u+v \\\\ 2u-v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the determinant of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=(-3)\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $det(A)>0,$ the affine transformation $T$ is orientation-preserving.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
